{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>As the stranger, so the native-born shall he be.</b> The foreigner who comes from another land and is separated from his land is called גר, from the language of גרגיר — a grain — that is separated from the tree; and one whose fathers are from the city is called אזרח, from the language of (Psalms 37:35) ״ומתערה כאזרח רענן״ — 'and takes root like a native sapling' — likened to a shoot planted from days of old. Therefore it says that this law shall be equal for all of them." "<b>For I am YHWH your Elohim.</b> If you upheld this law — 'I am YHWH your Elohim'; and from the affirmative you infer the negative, for anyone who does not uphold this law is as though he has removed himself from His divinity and denied the principle. For it is a well-known matter that all the beliefs the nations hold and their laws are the interpretation of the Torah, and the laws in the Torah are among the fundamental principles of the Torah; and when we do not engage in them, this is a profanation of the Name. And those who are able to protest and do not protest — they profane the Name and ascribe honor to idolatry and disparage the Torah of Moses and consume the money of Israel, for the whole world in its entirety depends on laws. And the Holy One, blessed be He, gave laws only to Israel, as it is said (Psalms 147:19–20): ״מגיד דבריו ליעקב חקיו ומשפטיו לישראל, לא עשה כן לכל גוי ומשפטים בל ידעום הללויה״ — 'He declares His words to Jacob, His statutes and judgments to Israel; He has not done so for any other nation, and as for judgments, they know them not, Hallelujah.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:11.175118019Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}